1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,360
In this tutorial we'll cover how live tasks work in Burp Suite, we'll show how you can set them up,

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,240
and we'll explain the live tasks that Burp creates by default. Live tasks are used to process traffic

3
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:28,640
from specific Burp tools and perform defined actions on them. Let's see how they work.

4
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:34,400
To create a live task, in the Burp Suite dashboard you click on the new live task button.

5
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:43,200
Here you can define the task type and the options are live audit which performs a vulnerability scan

6
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:51,680
or live passive crawl which adds entries to the target site map. You can define the tool scope and

7
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:58,320
this means the tools whose traffic will be inspected to select items that are processed by the live task.

8
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:05,920
You can choose proxy, repeater or intruder. Some common use cases might be to use proxy so that

9
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:12,240
you can use your browser to control which requests get sent for a vulnerability scan. If you want to

10
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:17,760
visit certain application endpoints, certain features, you can audit exactly the requests that result from

11
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:25,120
those. With repeater you might create a live task to add everything that you request through Burp repeater

12
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:32,720
to the target site map or for intruder you might create a live task to scrape all the responses that

13
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:40,720
come through intruder looking for new urls. You can configure the url scope and this defines which items

14
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:47,120
are processed by the live task based on their url. So you can configure the task to include everything

15
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:53,840
or to use the suite scope if you define one or you can define a custom scope just for this task.

16
00:01:55,040 --> 00:02:02,320
You can also configure whether to de-duplicate items that you've seen before based on their url and

17
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:08,400
parameter names. So for some live tasks it would be wasteful to repeat the same action again on the same

18
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:15,360
url and query string. So here you can configure not to do that. Under configuration you can set other options

19
00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:20,480
that are applicable to the live task and we'll look at those in a minute and you can also configure the

20
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:27,920
resource pool which affects how many requests can be made by this task in parallel if you've set that

21
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:33,760
up. The resource pool just works exactly the same as other types of tasks, other scans. So we're going

22
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:41,200
to set up a live audit task and to do that I'm going to select traffic through the proxy and I'm going to

23
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:50,240
define a custom scope for the task. I'm going to turn on deduplication

24
00:02:53,120 --> 00:03:01,200
and under configuration I'm going to select audit checks medium active. So I'm going to click OK to

25
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:08,640
create that task. You can see it appears in the dashboard here. So to see the task in action I'm going to open

26
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:20,960
burps embedded browser and I'm going to visit a url. I'm just going to turn off interception in the proxy

27
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:26,480
so that all the traffic gets passed through and now on the dashboard we can see the number of requests

28
00:03:26,480 --> 00:03:30,880
that have been made by this task and we can see the number of issues that have been reported.

29
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:36,320
If I browse around some of the urls

30
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:43,360
we can see more requests are happening and more issues are being reported.

31
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:51,040
If you click on view details you can see the full details for this task just as you can for any scan

32
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:56,080
task and you can drill right in and see all of the items that have been selected to be audited and you

33
00:03:56,080 --> 00:04:03,120
can monitor their progress. So this kind of task is really useful because it lets you use your browser

34
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:09,680
to guide burps scanner through parts of an application and just audit the items that you're interested in

35
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:14,240
just the items that you visit. So now let's look at how live passive crawl works.

36
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:20,000
So this task is used to add items to burps target site map.

37
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,960
So I'm going to select the proxy again and I'm going to define a custom scope.

38
00:04:30,480 --> 00:04:36,320
And in configuration, because this is a passive crawl task we have some different options and here

39
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:42,000
we can configure what types of item to add to the site map and which urls to add. So I'm just going

40
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:49,840
to leave those as defaults for now and create that task. So again we have an entry in the dashboard

41
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:54,800
showing this task for live passive crawl and to see how this works again I'm going to open the browser

42
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:56,720
and visit a URL.

43
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:10,960
And if I forward the traffic we can now see two responses have been processed and 15 items have

44
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:18,240
been added to the site map. If you go to the site map we can see that it's partly populated based on the

45
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:24,480
traffic that was processed through the proxy. The items that are showing in solid white have been

46
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:30,560
requested and all the other items have been inferred from responses passing through the proxy but haven't

47
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:36,640
yet been requested. If we look at the dashboard for the live passive crawl tasks we see there are two

48
00:05:36,640 --> 00:05:46,240
controls. One is to pause capturing and one is to pause the task itself. So if we pause the task

49
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:57,600
and make some more requests we can see we now have some responses queued. So these responses have been captured

50
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:03,120
and they're waiting to be processed but because we paused the task nothing's happening yet. So if we

51
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:10,320
unpause the task they get processed in the normal way. If instead we turn capturing off

52
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:19,680
and make some more requests you'll see we don't get any additional responses queued. So here we can just

53
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:25,280
turn off the capture and here we can turn off the processing of the items that have been captured.

54
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:33,200
When you create a live task there are some predefined tasks that can be instantly added into the configuration

55
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:39,360
of the wizard for common purposes which you can use directly or which you can customize further to meet

56
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:46,560
your needs. When BERT first opens in default settings you'll see that there are two live tasks that are

57
00:06:46,560 --> 00:06:53,520
created for you by default. So the first of these is a live passive crawl task which will add items to

58
00:06:53,520 --> 00:07:00,640
the sitemap as you browse. It adds links based on the item itself, any URLs observed on the same domain,

59
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:07,440
or any URLs that are in suite scope. And you also have a live audit task which just does purely

60
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:14,400
passive audit checks of all traffic. These default live tasks are replacements for features that

61
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:20,800
previously prior to BERT2 were implemented directly within the scanner tool and the proxy tool. These have

62
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:29,360
now been repurposed as general live tasks which you can edit and customize further. So that's how live tasks

63
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:48,160
work in BERT2 suite, how to set them up, and the live tasks that BERT2 creates by default.

